<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>





<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 10 Practice</title>

    <p workspace=".2in">
        <em>Clearly and correctly disprove a statement using a counterexample.
        </em>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Need to know</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        How to search for appropriate counterexamples (being strategic, e.g., trying odd/even, or 0,1,or 2 mod 3,
                        or positive/negative).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                     How to explain a counterexample, for example, how your example satisfies the hypothesis but not the conclusion.
                    </p>
                </li>

            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Practice From Class</title>

        <p>
            Weekly Practice 6: # 1,  Weekly Practice 1: Activity 3.4, Countrexamples you wrote up in the 
            proof portfolio.
        </p>

    </paragraphs>


    
<exercise>
            <introduction>
               <p> Each of thte following statements is incorrect. Show the statement is false using a counterexample. 
                You should clearly explain why the counterexample you found shows the statement is false.
 </p>
            </introduction>
    
                    <task>
                        <statement>
                        
                        <p>
                            
                           If <m>n</m> is an integer, then <m>n^{2} \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.
                                                       (Note - this statement is equivalent to <q> For all integers <m>n</m>,
                                                       <m>n^{2} \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.</q>)
                        </p>
            
                        </statement>
                        <solution>
                            <p>
                             
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For each integer <m>n</m>, <m>n^{2}+1</m> is a prime number.
                        </p>
                         </statement>
                         <solution>
